Step 2: Start Wireshark

Open using:

wireshark

OR open from Applications menu.

Step 3: Select Network Interface

Choose:

eth0, enp0s3 → Ethernet
wlan0 → Wi-Fi

Double-click the active interface.

Step 4: Apply ICMP Filter

In filter bar type:

icmp

Press Enter.

Now only ICMP packets appear.

Step 5: Generate ICMP Traffic

Open another terminal.

Run normal ping:

ping www.google.com

Let few packets generate.

Then stop using:

CTRL + C
Step 6: Generate Fragmented ICMP Packets

Now send larger packets:

ping -s 2000 www.yahoo.com

This creates packets larger than MTU and causes fragmentation.

Stop after few packets:

CTRL + C
Step 7: Stop Wireshark Capture

Click:

Red Stop button

Now analyze packets.

Understanding Packet Structure

Click any ICMP packet.

Expand:

Ethernet II
Internet Protocol Version 4
Internet Control Message Protocol
Answers
a) What is the IP address of your computer?

Open first:

ICMP Echo Request

Expand:

Internet Protocol Version 4

Look for:

Source Address

Example:

192.168.1.5

That is your computer’s IP address.

b) Upper layer protocol field value

In IPv4 header locate:

Protocol

Value will be:

1

Meaning:

ICMP

IP protocol numbers:

1 → ICMP
6 → TCP
17 → UDP
c) IP header bytes and payload bytes

Look for:

Header Length

Usually:

20 bytes

Payload size:

Use:

Total Length

Formula:

Payload = Total Length - Header Length

Example:

Field	Value
Total Length	84 bytes
Header Length	20 bytes

Payload:

84 - 20 = 64 bytes

So payload = 64 bytes.

d) Has the datagram been fragmented?

Check fields:

Flags
Fragment Offset

If:

Fragment Offset = 0
More Fragments = 0

then:

Packet is NOT fragmented.
e) Fields that always change between ICMP packets

These fields usually change:

Field	Reason
Identification	Unique packet ID
Header Checksum	Recalculated
TTL	May decrease
Sequence Number (ICMP)	Incremented
f) Fields that stay constant

Usually constant:

Field
Source IP
Destination IP
Protocol
Version
Header Length
g) After using ping -s 2000, has fragmentation occurred?

Yes.

Because Ethernet MTU is usually:

1500 bytes

A 2000-byte payload exceeds MTU.

Wireshark will show:

Multiple fragments
Same Identification field

You may see:

Fragmented IP protocol
h) First fragment details

Open first fragment.

Look for:

Flags → More Fragments = 1
Fragment Offset = 0

This indicates:

More fragments exist
This is first fragment

Total Length may be around:

1500 bytes

(depending on MTU)

Information indicating fragmentation

Look at:

Field	Meaning
More Fragments (MF) = 1	More fragments follow
Fragment Offset	Position of fragment
i) Information indicating NOT first fragment

For later fragments:

Fragment Offset > 0

Example:

Fragment Offset: 1480

This means:

Not first fragment

If:

MF = 1 → More fragments still exist
MF = 0 → Last fragment
j) Fields that change between first and second fragment

Changed fields:

Field	Reason
Fragment Offset	Different data position
Total Length	Fragment sizes differ
More Fragments Flag	May change
Header Checksum	Recomputed

Fields staying same:

Field
Source IP
Destination IP
Identification
How to Easily Identify Fragments in Wireshark

Use filter:

ip.flags.mf == 1 || ip.frag_offset > 0

This shows fragmented packets only.

